  "reasoning": "The patient presents with a nasty cough, cold, stuffy nose, sinus congestion, productive cough (coughing up mucus), chest pain upon coughing, loss of smell, and muscle aches. These are classic signs of an upper respiratory tract infection. Comparing these symptoms to the provided list and training references, they closely match Reference [1], which lists identical clinical features ('coughing a lot', 'nose is stuffy', 'lot of phlegm', 'coughing hurts my chest', 'can't smell anything', 'muscles hurt') and diagnoses it as a common cold. Other conditions were considered but ruled out: allergies typically lack productive cough and systemic muscle aches; pneumonia and bronchial asthma usually present with more severe respiratory distress, wheezing, or abnormal sputum color; and other systemic or dermatological conditions do not match the symptom profile. Therefore, the most accurate and factually supported diagnosis is common cold.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]common cold[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
